# OPALX issue #{{ISSUE}}: agent study workflow

Goal: give a coding agent a real OPALX bug, frozen at the commit right
before the real fix, and see whether it can find and fix it. Each run ends
in a real PR against `OPALX-project/OPALX`, and the agent never sees the
actual fix or any later commit history. The same issue is run many times,
possibly in parallel, by one or several models.

## Layout

```
opalx-issues/
  .physicscode/skills/          physicscode skills shared by all agent runs
                                (opalx-build-project, opalx-run-simulation,
                                push-fix-to-github)
  {{ISSUE}}/
    {{ISSUE}}-prompt.md         task prompt for the agent (hand-written, step 2)
    opalx-{{ISSUE}}-fix/        the workflow scripts (this folder)
    opalx-{{ISSUE}}-pr-template/  frozen snapshot + prebuilt builds (step 3)
    opalx-{{ISSUE}}-run-<run>/  transient working copy of one run (steps 4-6)
    results/
      original/                 ground truth from step 1, never shown to agents
      <run>/                    collected results of one run (step 6)
```

All scripts read the issue number from the folder name, so they take no
issue argument.

## Steps

1. **`bash fetch-issue.bash [pr-number]`** (already run). Writes to
   `results/original/`: the issue text with comments (`issue-{{ISSUE}}.md`),
   the diff of the PR that resolved it (`fix-{{ISSUE}}.diff`), and the fix
   commit and the base-branch commit right before it (`fix-{{ISSUE}}.commit`,
   `fix-{{ISSUE}}.base`). If it picked the wrong PR, re-run it with the PR
   number.
2. **Write `../{{ISSUE}}-prompt.md` by hand.** Use `opalx-issues/534/534-prompt.md`
   as the structural model: bug report, constraints, deliverables, task steps.
   - Describe observed vs. expected behavior as a user hitting the bug
     would. Never quote or summarize the diff. OPALX issues often already
     name the root cause and fix, so don't paste `issue-{{ISSUE}}.md` in
     verbatim.
   - Forbid `git pull`/`fetch`/`merge`/`rebase` and `--unshallow`: an
     unscoped pull unshallows origin and breaks the snapshot isolation.
     The permissions from step 4 enforce this too.
   - Delivery goes through the physicscode skill `push-fix-to-github`.
     The agent passes the issue number, a PR title and the PR body. The
     skill's script commits, pushes, opens the PR against
     `fix-{{ISSUE}}-sandbox`, and reads it back. Agents have no `gh`
     themselves, so don't ask them to verify with `gh pr view`. Instead,
     list the required body sections and tell them to check the body
     before calling the skill; re-running the skill updates the PR.
   - Make the skill call a mandatory final task step: "do not report
     completion until the script printed the PR URL". Pilot runs showed
     that agents otherwise stop before the PR.
   - Tell the agent its branch is already linked to the issue and that it
     must not create, rename or switch branches.
3. **`bash make-template.bash [fix-commit-sha]`**: builds
   `../opalx-{{ISSUE}}-pr-template/`, a frozen snapshot of `OPALX` +
   `opalx-manual` + `regression-tests-x` at the commit right before the
   fix.
   - Every fetch is a shallow fetch of one exact commit SHA, so no later
     commits land locally.
   - `OPALX` keeps its `origin`. The other two repos have none.
   - Pushes `fix-{{ISSUE}}-sandbox`, the shared PR base for all runs.
   - Builds OPALX twice (`build_serial`, `build_debug`), so runs start
     warm. A failed build only prints a warning.
4. **`bash new-agent-run.bash <run>`**: copies the template to
   `../opalx-{{ISSUE}}-run-<run>/` and rewrites the absolute paths in
   `build_*`. It creates the linked branch `{{ISSUE}}-fix-<run>` with
   `gh issue develop`, and checks that the sandbox is still at the template
   commit. It also writes:
   - `run-info.json`: base commit, branch.
   - `OPALX/physicscode.json`:
     - read-only access to the sibling repos and to
       `opalx-issues/.physicscode/skills` (wired in via `skills.paths`);
     - no web tools;
     - bash denies `gh`, `git push/fetch/pull/merge/rebase/remote/
       ls-remote/clone`, `--unshallow` and `curl`/`wget`. Only
       `push-fix.bash` reaches GitHub.
5. **`bash run-agent.bash <run> [model]`**: runs `physicscode` headless
   in the run's `OPALX/` with `{{ISSUE}}-prompt.md`. Claude Code skills are
   hidden via `PHYSICSCODE_DISABLE_CLAUDE_CODE_SKILLS=1`. It then:
   - adds model, start time, duration and exit status to `run-info.json`;
   - exports the session to `sessions/` (JSON + Markdown);
   - posts it as a gist and comments the link on the agent's PR.
6. **`bash collect-run.bash <run>`**: copies the session files into
   `results/<run>/`, together with `<run>.diff` (all changes vs. the base
   commit, committed or not) and `meta.json` (run info, head commit,
   commits, pushed commit, uncommitted files, diffstat, PR). Then it
   deletes the run's working copy.

## Multiple runs

All runs of this issue share `fix-{{ISSUE}}-sandbox`. Each run needs a
unique name. It gets its own working copy, its own branch
`{{ISSUE}}-fix-<run>` and its own open PR into the sandbox. Never merge
these PRs: the sandbox has to stay at the template commit, and
`new-agent-run.bash` refuses to continue otherwise. A run name can't be
reused while its remote branch exists. Runs can go in parallel, but run
them one after another if you measure latency.

```
bash new-agent-run.bash sonnet5-01
bash run-agent.bash     sonnet5-01 anthropic/claude-sonnet-5
bash collect-run.bash   sonnet5-01

bash new-agent-run.bash pai-120b-01
bash run-agent.bash     pai-120b-01 paidynamics/pai-120b
bash collect-run.bash   pai-120b-01
```
